 "drivingQuestion": "Why did Rav Naḥman need to state both rulings?",
 "outcome": "inconclusive",
 "outcomeNote": "The redundancy objection is answered in the next unit (yevamot.sugya425)",
 "continuesQuestionOf": null,
 "segments": [
  {
   "id": "yevamot.105b.13",
   "daf": "105b",
   "sentenceId": 12,
   "he": "חָלְצָה בִּשְׁנַיִם וְכוּ׳. אָמַר רַב יוֹסֵף בַּר מִנְיוֹמֵי אָמַר רַב נַחְמָן: אֵין הֲלָכָה כְּאוֹתוֹ הַזּוּג. וְהָא אָמַר רַב נַחְמָן חֲדָא זִימְנָא. דְּאָמַר רַב יוֹסֵף בַּר מִנְיוֹמֵי אָמַר רַב נַחְמָן: חֲלִיצָה בִּשְׁלֹשָׁה!",
   "en": "It was taught in the mishna: «If she performed ḥalitza before two» or three people, and one of them is found to be disqualified to serve as a judge, Rabbi Shimon and Rabbi Yoḥanan the Cobbler validate it. «Rav Yosef bar Minyumi said» that «Rav Naḥman said: The halakha does not follow this pair» who validate such a case. The Gemara asks: «But didn’t Rav Naḥman» already «say this» same ruling «one time» before? «As Rav Yosef bar Minyumi said» that «Rav Naḥman said: Ḥalitza must be» conducted «before three» people, indicating that there must be no fewer than three valid judges."
  }
 ],
